1
00:00:36,467 --> 00:00:38,990
This is our
planet's hothouse

2
00:00:39,030 --> 00:00:40,151
the jungle

3
00:00:40,191 --> 00:00:42,273
the tropical rainforest

4
00:00:45,078 --> 00:00:48,922
Forests like
these occupy only 3% of the land

5
00:00:48,962 --> 00:00:52,766
yet they're home
to over half of the world's species

6
00:00:54,584 --> 00:00:58,390
But how do so many different kinds of plants and animals

7
00:00:58,430 --> 00:01:02,194
find the space
here to live alongside one another？

8
00:01:14,691 --> 00:01:15,452
On the dark

9
00:01:15,492 --> 00:01:17,094
humid forest floor

10
00:01:17,134 --> 00:01:19,817
the jungle
appears to be lifeless

11
00:01:21,719 --> 00:01:25,523
Often the only
signs of life are what you hear

12
00:01:32,291 --> 00:01:36,815
A male bluebird
of paradise is advertising for a mate

13
00:01:39,139 --> 00:01:40,700
It's quite a
performance

14
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:43,423
But he's not the
only bird of paradise here

15
00:01:43,463 --> 00:01:45,545
Keen to make
an impression

16
00:01:47,211 --> 00:01:51,133
There are nearly
40 different kinds on the island of New Guinea

17
00:01:51,173 --> 00:01:55,374
Each with a display seemingly more bizarre than the rest

18
00:02:04,298 --> 00:02:06,739
A rifle bird
of paradise

19
00:02:17,147 --> 00:02:19,030
Like many
jungle animals

20
00:02:19,070 --> 00:02:22,393
birds of paradise avoid competing with each other

21
00:02:22,433 --> 00:02:27,638
And these do so by living in different parts of this junglecovered island

22
00:02:33,525 --> 00:02:35,927
The sixprumed
bird of paradise

23
00:02:35,967 --> 00:02:39,851
displays in his
special clearing on the forest floor

24
00:02:47,185 --> 00:02:52,427
The magnificent bird of paradise favours the low branches of bushes

25
00:02:56,429 --> 00:02:59,591
Its female is
modestly dressed

26
00:03:11,789 --> 00:03:14,352
The male has a
good set of lungs

27
00:03:14,392 --> 00:03:17,475
But he'll have to
do more than flutter his eyelids

28
00:03:17,515 --> 00:03:19,476
If he wants
to impress her

29
00:03:26,204 --> 00:03:29,007
It'll all depend
on his performance

30
00:03:33,773 --> 00:03:35,775
The females may
be dull looking

31
00:03:35,815 --> 00:03:37,576
But they're
very picky

32
00:03:39,773 --> 00:03:43,013
And it's time for
a really close inspection

33
00:03:48,893 --> 00:03:50,733
His right
side looks fine

34
00:03:50,773 --> 00:03:52,572
but what
about his left？

35
00:03:57,812 --> 00:03:59,052
Pretty impressive

36
00:03:59,092 --> 00:04:01,892
but is he
magnificent enough？

37
00:04:04,243 --> 00:04:05,364
Oh dear

38
00:04:05,404 --> 00:04:07,486
her departure says it all

39
00:04:08,208 --> 00:04:10,651
Generations of
choosy females

40
00:04:10,691 --> 00:04:15,056
Have driven the
evolution of these remarkable displays

41
00:04:15,858 --> 00:04:18,101
The more
extravagant a male is

42
00:04:18,141 --> 00:04:20,504
The more likely
he will be noticed

43
00:04:46,430 --> 00:04:49,832
New Guinea lies
in a warm tropical belt

44
00:04:49,872 --> 00:04:52,913
that girdles our
planet around the equator

45
00:04:53,314 --> 00:04:57,476
With abundant
rainfall and 12 hours of daylight

46
00:04:57,516 --> 00:04:59,277
365 days a year

47
00:04:59,317 --> 00:05:02,399
It's here that
rainforests flourish

48
00:05:08,182 --> 00:05:09,223
Surprisingly

49
00:05:09,263 --> 00:05:14,146
only 2% of the
sunlight filters down to the forest floor

50
00:05:16,549 --> 00:05:17,469
Down here

51
00:05:17,509 --> 00:05:20,271
seedlings
struggle to grow

52
00:05:21,032 --> 00:05:24,034
But the gloom
is not eternal

53
00:05:57,673 --> 00:06:01,877
The death of a
forest giant is always saddening

54
00:06:01,917 --> 00:06:03,479
But it has
to happen

55
00:06:03,519 --> 00:06:06,160
if the forest is
to remain healthy

56
00:06:07,974 --> 00:06:10,537
The sudden
blaze of sunlight

57
00:06:10,577 --> 00:06:13,459
will bring life
to the forest floor

58
00:06:17,424 --> 00:06:19,626
A single
hectare of rainforest

59
00:06:19,666 --> 00:06:23,711
may contain as
many as 250 species of tree

60
00:06:23,751 --> 00:06:27,314
That's nearly ten
times the number that grow in Britain

61
00:06:27,354 --> 00:06:30,478
And the thirst
for light triggers a race

62
00:06:30,518 --> 00:06:32,279
for a
place in the sun

63
00:06:48,212 --> 00:06:50,212
There's no
time to waste

64
00:06:50,252 --> 00:06:53,491
A seed that may
have fallen only a few days ago

65
00:06:53,531 --> 00:06:56,531
now burst
through the leaf litter

66
00:07:11,043 --> 00:07:13,084
With so many
competitors

67
00:07:13,124 --> 00:07:16,165
getting a good
start is critical

68
00:07:18,567 --> 00:07:22,289
But each plant
has its own particular strategy

69
00:07:22,329 --> 00:07:25,851
For making the
most of this rare opportunity

70
00:07:26,888 --> 00:07:29,850
The seeds of
hardwoods are quick to germinate

71
00:07:29,890 --> 00:07:31,932
But like the
fabled tortoise

72
00:07:31,972 --> 00:07:35,294
their strategy is
to be slow and steady

73
00:07:40,098 --> 00:07:42,139
Vines and
other climbers

74
00:07:42,179 --> 00:07:45,622
Put all their
energy into rapid vertical growth

75
00:07:45,662 --> 00:07:47,263
rather than girth

76
00:07:47,503 --> 00:07:50,225
Though they'll
need to be well supported

77
00:07:56,550 --> 00:07:58,994
The climbers'
strategy looks chaotic

78
00:07:59,034 --> 00:08:01,157
But there's
method in their madness

79
00:08:01,197 --> 00:08:04,202
Their growing
tips circle like lassoes

80
00:08:04,242 --> 00:08:07,205
Searching out
anchors for their spindly stems

81
00:08:14,632 --> 00:08:16,955
They put coils
in their tendrils

82
00:08:16,995 --> 00:08:19,038
so that if
their support moves

83
00:08:19,078 --> 00:08:21,440
they will
stretch and not snap

84
00:08:24,125 --> 00:08:26,368
But the front
runners at this stage

85
00:08:26,408 --> 00:08:28,250
the first to
fill the clearing

86
00:08:28,290 --> 00:08:31,374
are pioneers
like the macaranga's

87
00:08:32,456 --> 00:08:36,261
Their immense
leaves capture huge amounts of sunlight

88
00:08:36,301 --> 00:08:38,544
so fueling
their growth

89
00:08:44,964 --> 00:08:45,884
As a result

90
00:08:45,924 --> 00:08:49,565
the macaranga
grow a remarkable eight metres a year

91
00:08:49,605 --> 00:08:52,925
surging ahead of
almost all their rivals

92
00:09:05,967 --> 00:09:08,128
In the race
for the top spot

93
00:09:08,168 --> 00:09:09,808
Hundreds
will start

94
00:09:09,848 --> 00:09:12,568
Yet few will ever
reach the finishing line

95
00:09:12,608 --> 00:09:16,368
Their growth cut
short by the diminishing light

96
00:09:19,289 --> 00:09:21,329
In less
than four years

97
00:09:21,369 --> 00:09:23,289
The gap
will have gone

98
00:09:23,649 --> 00:09:26,410
But that's not
the end of the race

99
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:38,761
The ultimate
winners are the tortoises

100
00:09:38,801 --> 00:09:41,643
the slow and
steady hardwoods

101
00:09:43,804 --> 00:09:47,285
When the shortlived pioneers have fallen

102
00:09:47,326 --> 00:09:50,327
it's the hardwoods that take their place

103
00:09:50,447 --> 00:09:53,249
And a 50 metre
giant like this one

104
00:09:53,289 --> 00:09:55,290
may keep its
place in the sun

105
00:09:55,330 --> 00:09:58,171
for another 200 years

106
00:10:35,748 --> 00:10:37,668
At the top
is the canopy

107
00:10:37,708 --> 00:10:40,268
the engine
room of the jungle

108
00:10:40,308 --> 00:10:44,388
It's up here that
most of the animal life in the rainforest

109
00:10:44,428 --> 00:10:45,868
can be found

110
00:10:46,828 --> 00:10:51,188
But despite the
apparent abundance of vegetable food

111
00:10:51,348 --> 00:10:53,948
gathering it
is seldom easy

112
00:11:08,069 --> 00:11:10,351
With no
real seasons

113
00:11:10,391 --> 00:11:14,596
each tree flowers
or fruits at a different time of the year

114
00:11:14,636 --> 00:11:18,559
which means that
food is very widely spaced

115
00:11:24,062 --> 00:11:26,262
Monkeys like
these tamarins

116
00:11:26,302 --> 00:11:29,502
must search the
canopy for all kinds of food

117
00:11:29,542 --> 00:11:31,262
if they're
to survive

118
00:11:34,182 --> 00:11:37,022
But across the
world's rainforests

119
00:11:37,062 --> 00:11:39,302
there's one type
of fruiting tree

120
00:11:39,342 --> 00:11:41,542
that always delivers

121
00:11:41,662 --> 00:11:42,822
The fig

122
00:11:52,625 --> 00:11:54,104
Wherever they grow

123
00:11:54,144 --> 00:11:55,864
figs are a magnet

124
00:11:55,904 --> 00:11:59,104
for a great
diversity of animals

125
00:12:01,344 --> 00:12:02,503
In the Amazon

126
00:12:02,543 --> 00:12:04,023
the first
to appear

127
00:12:04,063 --> 00:12:06,063
are the
spider monkeys

128
00:12:07,463 --> 00:12:09,102
These
large primates

129
00:12:09,142 --> 00:12:10,782
are big fig eaters

130
00:12:10,822 --> 00:12:13,702
But they won't
have the tree to themselves for long

131
00:12:13,742 --> 00:12:15,862
Others will
want to share

132
00:12:17,381 --> 00:12:20,341
Like the diminutive emperor tamarins

133
00:12:24,108 --> 00:12:26,230
The tamarins
love figs too

134
00:12:26,270 --> 00:12:30,034
But being petite
means they're easily scared off

135
00:12:40,449 --> 00:12:42,812
Squirrel
monkeys are also small

136
00:12:42,852 --> 00:12:45,495
But they have
strength in numbers

137
00:12:48,177 --> 00:12:50,857
Their timeshare
on the tree may be short

138
00:12:50,897 --> 00:12:53,976
so their tactics
are more smash and grab

139
00:13:03,374 --> 00:13:06,894
Capuchin monkeys
are the bully boys in these forests

140
00:13:06,934 --> 00:13:09,933
And they want the
ripe figs for themselves

141
00:13:27,891 --> 00:13:31,935
Figs are one of
the few trees that fruit the year round

142
00:13:32,576 --> 00:13:35,019
So when other
food is scarce

143
00:13:35,059 --> 00:13:39,142
these fruits are
always available somewhere or other

144
00:13:43,348 --> 00:13:46,033
Even for leaf
eaters like howler monkeys

145
00:13:46,073 --> 00:13:49,277
the ripe figs are
just too good to miss

146
00:13:55,368 --> 00:14:00,014
And howlers are
too big for the capuchins to chase off

147
00:14:03,987 --> 00:14:05,591
Figs are
so popular

148
00:14:05,631 --> 00:14:09,642
that as many as
44 different kinds of bird and monkey

149
00:14:09,682 --> 00:14:12,249
Have been seen
working a shift system

150
00:14:12,289 --> 00:14:14,092
on a single tree

151
00:14:23,426 --> 00:14:26,346
Because fruiting
trees are so valuable

152
00:14:26,386 --> 00:14:29,225
many monkeys
are territorial

153
00:14:29,425 --> 00:14:31,265
And if you live
in the treetops

154
00:14:31,305 --> 00:14:34,904
There's perhaps no better way of staking your claim to a territory

155
00:14:34,944 --> 00:14:36,104
Than this

156
00:14:42,801 --> 00:14:46,804
The calls of the
siamang gibbons begin as a duet

157
00:14:46,844 --> 00:14:49,446
between the
dominant male and female

158
00:15:02,176 --> 00:15:04,577
The rest of their
family soon join in

159
00:15:04,617 --> 00:15:07,978
and it results in
a frenzy of activity

160
00:15:17,022 --> 00:15:20,023
The calls can
carry over a mile

161
00:15:20,103 --> 00:15:22,224
and their
message is clear

162
00:15:27,346 --> 00:15:29,948
They tell any
neighbouring siamangs

163
00:15:29,988 --> 00:15:31,789
「This is
our territory

164
00:15:31,829 --> 00:15:33,030
keep out」

165
00:15:43,238 --> 00:15:43,878
Up here

166
00:15:43,918 --> 00:15:45,960
the calls of
siamang gibbons

167
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,601
seem to
dominate the airwaves

168
00:15:48,802 --> 00:15:51,524
But with the
jungle's incredible diversity

169
00:15:51,564 --> 00:15:54,685
There are countless others trying to be heard too

170
00:16:01,371 --> 00:16:05,574
Every layer seems
to beat to a different tune

171
00:16:23,351 --> 00:16:24,711
In the
early morning

172
00:16:24,751 --> 00:16:28,114
the forest's
chorus is particularly rich

173
00:16:28,154 --> 00:16:31,275
Sounds travel
further in the cooler air

174
00:16:35,799 --> 00:16:38,961
But few calls can
penetrate as far

175
00:16:39,001 --> 00:16:42,203
through the dense
vegetation as this one

176
00:16:42,243 --> 00:16:46,446
The deep bass
solo of a male orangutan

177
00:17:02,332 --> 00:17:03,935
In the
middle of the day

178
00:17:03,975 --> 00:17:05,898
little stirs
in the jungle

179
00:17:05,938 --> 00:17:06,739
And the hot

180
00:17:06,779 --> 00:17:09,742
dense air
muffled the sound

181
00:17:13,132 --> 00:17:15,055
As the
afternoon wears on

182
00:17:15,095 --> 00:17:18,339
a different set
of players begin to warm up

183
00:17:20,944 --> 00:17:22,787
Insects
work in harmony

184
00:17:22,827 --> 00:17:26,712
timing their calls to fall between the notes of others

185
00:17:35,069 --> 00:17:38,390
Many singers
stick to precise schedules

186
00:17:38,430 --> 00:17:39,950
And right on cue

187
00:17:39,990 --> 00:17:42,070
the six o'
clock cicada

188
00:17:54,792 --> 00:17:58,632
Night brings out
a whole new orchestra

189
00:18:23,268 --> 00:18:28,067
The cacophony of
competing calls seems deafening to us

190
00:18:28,107 --> 00:18:33,707
But frogs' ears are tuned to hear only the calls of their own kind

191
00:18:41,426 --> 00:18:45,266
Songs of courtship echo from all around

192
00:18:58,508 --> 00:19:03,628
Male gliding leaf
frogs leap from the treetops

193
00:19:39,001 --> 00:19:40,802
To slow
their descent

194
00:19:40,842 --> 00:19:43,643
they use their
huge webbed feet

195
00:19:43,683 --> 00:19:45,324
as parachutes

196
00:19:50,137 --> 00:19:52,461
These large
tree frogs

197
00:19:52,501 --> 00:19:55,225
spend most of
their lives in the high canopy

198
00:19:55,265 --> 00:19:58,669
And only come
down when it's time to breed

199
00:20:04,279 --> 00:20:05,401
Once settled

200
00:20:05,441 --> 00:20:09,767
they begin to
serenade their unseen females

201
00:20:21,439 --> 00:20:22,000
Now

202
00:20:22,040 --> 00:20:25,685
it's time for the
females to make their move

203
00:20:30,198 --> 00:20:32,319
There's no
shortage of suitors

204
00:20:32,359 --> 00:20:35,399
But this female
has already made her choice

205
00:20:35,439 --> 00:20:38,360
She's heading
towards the loudest call

206
00:20:38,400 --> 00:20:41,681
Because loud
calls come from big frogs

207
00:20:41,961 --> 00:20:44,122
And big is best

208
00:20:44,762 --> 00:20:45,642
But to reach him

209
00:20:45,682 --> 00:20:49,563
she must run the
gauntlet of a gang of smaller suitors

210
00:20:50,123 --> 00:20:52,004
Their only
chance of mating

211
00:20:52,044 --> 00:20:54,965
is to make a
sneaky interception

212
00:21:00,658 --> 00:21:02,098
He scored

213
00:21:02,259 --> 00:21:04,903
But with more
females arriving all the time

214
00:21:04,943 --> 00:21:09,067
It's not over
until the fat frog stops singing

215
00:21:15,755 --> 00:21:16,316
Feet

216
00:21:16,356 --> 00:21:18,118
so vital
for gliding

217
00:21:18,158 --> 00:21:20,921
are now put
to other uses

218
00:21:26,415 --> 00:21:28,016
Two's company

219
00:21:28,257 --> 00:21:30,899
three's
inconvenient

220
00:21:31,060 --> 00:21:32,221
But in any case

221
00:21:32,261 --> 00:21:35,864
all male frogs
are equipped with dry thumbs

222
00:21:35,904 --> 00:21:38,547
which enable them
to get a viselike grip

223
00:21:38,587 --> 00:21:40,829
on their
moist partners

224
00:21:42,111 --> 00:21:44,153
It's a case
of first come

225
00:21:44,193 --> 00:21:45,834
first served

226
00:21:54,219 --> 00:21:56,740
Living in such a
humid environment

227
00:21:56,780 --> 00:22:01,182
means jungle frogs are less tied to puddles and pools

228
00:22:01,222 --> 00:22:04,783
And these even
lay their eggs out of water

229
00:22:14,588 --> 00:22:17,429
There's little
chance of them drying out

230
00:22:17,550 --> 00:22:18,390
And up here

231
00:22:18,430 --> 00:22:20,671
they're safer
from predators

232
00:22:28,750 --> 00:22:29,871
Surprisingly

233
00:22:29,911 --> 00:22:33,514
it doesn't rain
every day in the rainforest

234
00:22:34,235 --> 00:22:38,038
But more still
falls here than anywhere else on Earth

235
00:22:38,439 --> 00:22:39,280
On average

236
00:22:39,320 --> 00:22:41,402
over two
metres a year

237
00:22:51,695 --> 00:22:57,182
A single tree can
suck up hundreds of tons of water each year

238
00:23:00,148 --> 00:23:02,672
But the trees can'
t use all this water

239
00:23:02,712 --> 00:23:05,997
So much of it
returns to the air as vapour

240
00:23:06,037 --> 00:23:08,760
forming
mist and clouds

241
00:23:14,645 --> 00:23:15,924
In the Amazon

242
00:23:15,964 --> 00:23:19,724
the largest unbroken stretch of rainforest in the world

243
00:23:19,764 --> 00:23:22,523
Half of all the
rainwater that falls

244
00:23:22,563 --> 00:23:26,362
comes from clouds
produced by the trees themselves

245
00:23:59,391 --> 00:24:01,352
With so much rain

246
00:24:01,392 --> 00:24:07,354
It's not surprising that many of the world's largest rivers are found in rainforests

247
00:24:23,136 --> 00:24:24,777
Inside the forest

248
00:24:24,817 --> 00:24:26,299
the high humidity

249
00:24:26,339 --> 00:24:28,501
creates the
perfect conditions

250
00:24:28,541 --> 00:24:30,182
for a
strange world

251
00:24:30,222 --> 00:24:33,225
where life is
built on decay

252
00:24:46,359 --> 00:24:50,884
Amoebalike slime
moulds cruise the surface

253
00:24:50,924 --> 00:24:54,729
feeding on
bacteria and rotting vegetation

254
00:25:11,097 --> 00:25:14,901
Fungi also
flourish on decay

255
00:25:16,584 --> 00:25:19,909
These are the
fruiting bodies of the fungi

256
00:25:19,949 --> 00:25:21,791
The only
visible sign

257
00:25:21,831 --> 00:25:24,355
Of a vast
underground network

258
00:25:24,395 --> 00:25:26,517
Of fungal
filaments

259
00:25:58,577 --> 00:26:00,220
In temperate forests

260
00:26:00,260 --> 00:26:04,508
the buildup of
leaf litter creates rich stores of nutrients

261
00:26:04,548 --> 00:26:07,232
That however
doesn't happen here

262
00:26:11,326 --> 00:26:15,453
Nutrients that
reach the soil are leached out by the rain

263
00:26:15,493 --> 00:26:20,181
But fungi are connected to tree roots by their underground filaments

264
00:26:20,221 --> 00:26:22,384
And by quickly
consuming the dead

265
00:26:22,424 --> 00:26:25,389
they help to
recycle crucial minerals

266
00:26:25,429 --> 00:26:27,912
Straight back
into the trees

267
00:26:34,564 --> 00:26:38,144
And this recycling happens faster here

268
00:26:38,184 --> 00:26:40,715
than anywhere
else on the planet

269
00:27:00,949 --> 00:27:06,312
There are thought to be nearly a million different types of fungi in the tropics

270
00:27:06,352 --> 00:27:10,234
The vast majority
still unknown to science

271
00:27:17,038 --> 00:27:19,599
But one thing'
s for certain

272
00:27:19,639 --> 00:27:21,040
Without fungi

273
00:27:21,080 --> 00:27:24,001
rainforests
could not exist

274
00:27:35,859 --> 00:27:38,822
Nothing goes to
waste in a rainforest

275
00:27:38,943 --> 00:27:41,185
The fungi become
food for others

276
00:27:41,225 --> 00:27:43,346
like these
beetle larvae

277
00:27:48,192 --> 00:27:51,435
Finding the fungus isn't a problem for the grubs

278
00:27:51,475 --> 00:27:55,279
Since their caring parents actually show them the way

279
00:28:09,299 --> 00:28:10,700
Incredibly

280
00:28:11,261 --> 00:28:14,543
80% of all
insects live in jungles

281
00:28:14,944 --> 00:28:17,506
Few are more
successful than the ants

282
00:28:17,546 --> 00:28:21,669
There can be 8
million individuals in a single hectare

283
00:28:23,150 --> 00:28:26,953
But jungle ants
don't have it all their own way

284
00:28:34,032 --> 00:28:38,956
These bullet ants
are showing some worrying symptoms

285
00:28:39,998 --> 00:28:43,842
Spores from a
parasitic fungus called Cordyceps

286
00:28:43,882 --> 00:28:47,845
Have infiltrated
their bodies and their minds

287
00:28:54,532 --> 00:28:59,176
Its infected
brain directs this ant upwards

288
00:29:00,897 --> 00:29:03,216
Then utterly
disorientated

289
00:29:03,256 --> 00:29:06,056
it grips the
stem with its mandibles

290
00:29:06,736 --> 00:29:07,976
Those afflicted

291
00:29:08,016 --> 00:29:09,856
that are
discovered by the workers

292
00:29:09,896 --> 00:29:11,896
are quickly
taken away and dumped

293
00:29:11,936 --> 00:29:14,136
far away
from the colony

294
00:29:15,936 --> 00:29:17,576
It seems extreme

295
00:29:17,616 --> 00:29:20,095
but this is
the reason why

296
00:29:22,317 --> 00:29:25,199
Like something
out of science fiction

297
00:29:25,319 --> 00:29:30,482
the fruiting body
of the cordyceps erupts from the ant's head

298
00:29:45,666 --> 00:29:48,228
It can take
three weeks to grow

299
00:29:48,389 --> 00:29:49,510
And when finished

300
00:29:49,550 --> 00:29:53,193
the deadly spores
will burst from its tip

301
00:29:53,354 --> 00:29:53,995
Then

302
00:29:54,035 --> 00:29:58,238
any ant in the
vicinity will be in serious risk of death

303
00:30:01,006 --> 00:30:02,807
The fungus
is so virulent

304
00:30:02,847 --> 00:30:05,769
it can wipe out
whole colonies of ants

305
00:30:06,169 --> 00:30:10,012
And it's not just
ants that fall victim to this killer

306
00:30:12,214 --> 00:30:17,138
There are literally thousands of different types of cordyceps fungi

307
00:30:17,178 --> 00:30:18,379
and remarkably

308
00:30:18,419 --> 00:30:22,581
each specialises
on just one species

309
00:30:59,112 --> 00:31:04,234
But these attacks
do have a positive effect on the jungle's diversity

310
00:31:04,274 --> 00:31:06,394
since
parasites like these

311
00:31:06,434 --> 00:31:10,515
stop any one
group of animal getting the upper hand

312
00:31:11,356 --> 00:31:13,677
The more numerous
a species becomes

313
00:31:13,717 --> 00:31:17,118
the more likely
it will be attacked by its nemesis

314
00:31:17,158 --> 00:31:19,118
a cordyceps fungus

315
00:31:43,550 --> 00:31:45,871
With so much
competition

316
00:31:45,911 --> 00:31:49,352
jungles have
become the home of the specialist

317
00:31:49,752 --> 00:31:50,793
And this animal

318
00:31:50,833 --> 00:31:52,513
in the
island of Borneo

319
00:31:52,553 --> 00:31:54,634
is one of the
most unusual

320
00:31:55,754 --> 00:31:57,075
It's a colugo

321
00:31:57,115 --> 00:31:58,675
or flying lemur

322
00:31:58,715 --> 00:32:00,956
though this is
something of a misnomer

323
00:32:00,996 --> 00:32:02,717
As it doesn't
actually fly

324
00:32:02,757 --> 00:32:05,197
and it certainly isn't a lemur

325
00:32:05,278 --> 00:32:05,758
In fact

326
00:32:05,798 --> 00:32:09,879
nobody's quite
sure who its closest relative is

327
00:32:29,908 --> 00:32:33,914
The collugo
depends on a diet of young leaves

328
00:32:33,954 --> 00:32:35,477
And to find
enough of them

329
00:32:35,517 --> 00:32:38,240
it must move
from tree to tree

330
00:32:40,204 --> 00:32:42,768
The leaves are
not very nutritious

331
00:32:42,808 --> 00:32:46,653
But then getting
around doesn't use much energy

332
00:32:52,698 --> 00:32:54,058
In a single night

333
00:32:54,098 --> 00:32:57,657
a Kolugo might
have to travel as far as two miles

334
00:32:57,697 --> 00:32:59,977
But that task
is made easier

335
00:33:00,017 --> 00:33:02,976
by its superior
gliding skills

336
00:33:35,392 --> 00:33:40,194
The secret of success in the competitive jungle is specialising

337
00:33:40,234 --> 00:33:44,756
and this has led to the evolution of some very intimate relationships

338
00:33:44,796 --> 00:33:47,397
between
plants and animals

339
00:33:49,038 --> 00:33:51,279
These are
pitcher plants

340
00:33:51,319 --> 00:33:53,039
also from Borneo

341
00:33:58,601 --> 00:34:02,760
Adapted to living
in very low nutrient soils

342
00:34:02,800 --> 00:34:06,240
the pitcher plant
gets most of its nourishment from insects

343
00:34:06,280 --> 00:34:08,320
lured to
nectar glands

344
00:34:08,360 --> 00:34:10,879
on the underside of the lids

345
00:34:12,279 --> 00:34:13,519
Once on board

346
00:34:13,559 --> 00:34:15,559
the waxy sides
of the pitcher

347
00:34:15,599 --> 00:34:18,198
ensure little
chance of escape

348
00:34:21,958 --> 00:34:25,157
Most slip to
a watery grave

349
00:34:33,229 --> 00:34:37,674
At the bottom of
the pitcher glands secrete enzymes

350
00:34:37,714 --> 00:34:40,356
which help to
digest the corpses

351
00:34:40,396 --> 00:34:42,478
so feeding
the plant

352
00:34:43,520 --> 00:34:47,483
But not all visitors have a fatal attraction to the pitchers

353
00:34:48,925 --> 00:34:53,210
The red crab spider spends its entire life in the pitchers

354
00:34:53,250 --> 00:34:56,052
hanging on with
threads of silk

355
00:35:05,690 --> 00:35:07,570
Instead of
building a web

356
00:35:07,610 --> 00:35:11,651
it relies on the
waterfilled pitcher to trap its food

357
00:35:14,492 --> 00:35:16,172
When an
ant falls in

358
00:35:16,212 --> 00:35:19,493
the spider
simply waits for it to drown

359
00:35:19,613 --> 00:35:23,494
and then abseils
down for a spot of fishing

360
00:35:30,800 --> 00:35:31,600
Alive

361
00:35:31,640 --> 00:35:35,241
this ant would be
far too dangerous for the spider to tackle

362
00:35:35,281 --> 00:35:38,082
So using the
pitchers as traps

363
00:35:38,122 --> 00:35:40,882
means it can
get bigger meals

364
00:35:43,283 --> 00:35:46,404
And the spider
doesn't rob the pitcher of everything

365
00:35:46,444 --> 00:35:50,485
The digested remains of its booty will end up in the water

366
00:35:50,525 --> 00:35:53,645
providing instant
food for the plant

367
00:35:58,188 --> 00:36:00,791
Other food like
mosquito larvae

368
00:36:00,831 --> 00:36:02,794
seems to be
out of reach

369
00:36:02,834 --> 00:36:06,117
But the spider
has another surprising trick

370
00:36:06,358 --> 00:36:09,682
By taking its own
air supply trapped in a bubble

371
00:36:09,722 --> 00:36:14,447
The crab spider can actually dive to the very bottom of the pitcher

372
00:36:18,172 --> 00:36:19,934
Once the
prey is captured

373
00:36:19,974 --> 00:36:24,779
the spider hauls
itself back up its silken safety line

374
00:36:32,354 --> 00:36:36,394
The pitcher is a
onestop shop for this spider

375
00:36:36,434 --> 00:36:38,195
But it's not alone

376
00:36:38,435 --> 00:36:39,155
In the jungle

377
00:36:39,195 --> 00:36:41,076
there's competition for everything

378
00:36:41,116 --> 00:36:44,236
Even a small
waterfilled pitcher plant

379
00:37:00,827 --> 00:37:05,788
Such specialists
create the jungle's remarkable diversity

380
00:37:05,948 --> 00:37:08,069
But finding
enough food to survive

381
00:37:08,109 --> 00:37:09,349
is so challenging

382
00:37:09,389 --> 00:37:12,830
that most animals
living here tend to be small

383
00:37:12,870 --> 00:37:15,270
Though there
are exceptions

384
00:37:23,096 --> 00:37:25,498
This is the Congo

385
00:37:25,539 --> 00:37:26,740
in Africa

386
00:37:26,780 --> 00:37:28,903
It's a vast
wilderness

387
00:37:28,943 --> 00:37:32,346
and the least
explored of all jungles

388
00:37:34,870 --> 00:37:35,831
From up here

389
00:37:35,871 --> 00:37:41,237
the forest looks similar to the ones that grow in the Amazon or Southeast Asia

390
00:37:41,558 --> 00:37:42,720
But down below

391
00:37:42,760 --> 00:37:45,643
there are some
unexpected sights

392
00:38:03,790 --> 00:38:05,792
Crisscrossing this forest

393
00:38:05,832 --> 00:38:08,754
are countless
miles of highways

394
00:38:09,155 --> 00:38:12,357
And they were
made by something big

395
00:38:22,013 --> 00:38:27,294
Forest elephants
roam great distances in their search for food

396
00:38:27,454 --> 00:38:28,854
But to survive

397
00:38:28,894 --> 00:38:32,374
they must emerge
from the gloom of the forest

398
00:38:34,294 --> 00:38:36,494
And clearings
like this one

399
00:38:36,534 --> 00:38:40,255
are a magnet for
elephants from far and wide

400
00:39:36,793 --> 00:39:39,838
These elephants
live in much smaller groups

401
00:39:39,878 --> 00:39:42,121
than their
savannah cousins

402
00:39:42,843 --> 00:39:44,646
This might be
the first time

403
00:39:44,686 --> 00:39:47,131
that one group
will have seen another

404
00:39:47,171 --> 00:39:48,412
for a month

405
00:39:58,330 --> 00:39:59,853
For the
adult males

406
00:39:59,893 --> 00:40:01,335
it's a
welcome break

407
00:40:01,375 --> 00:40:05,180
in an otherwise
largely solitary existence

408
00:40:13,634 --> 00:40:17,559
And they're not
the only animals attracted to the clearing

409
00:40:20,172 --> 00:40:23,576
Forest buffaloes
and Red River hogs

410
00:40:23,616 --> 00:40:26,137
are also
regular visitors

411
00:40:26,378 --> 00:40:27,819
As are bongos

412
00:40:27,859 --> 00:40:31,462
which are very
difficult to see outside these clearings

413
00:40:32,424 --> 00:40:34,545
All these large
forest animals

414
00:40:34,585 --> 00:40:38,549
have come here to
collect an essential element of their diet

415
00:40:38,589 --> 00:40:41,271
that lies buried
beneath the mud

416
00:40:46,238 --> 00:40:47,999
And the
elephant's trunk

417
00:40:48,039 --> 00:40:50,802
is the perfect
tool for reaching it

418
00:41:05,577 --> 00:41:06,979
To get
what they seek

419
00:41:07,019 --> 00:41:10,022
the prospecting
elephants must first blow away

420
00:41:10,062 --> 00:41:12,224
the covering
layer of silt

421
00:41:34,874 --> 00:41:37,333
Satisfaction at last

422
00:41:42,581 --> 00:41:45,703
They're collecting a particular kind of clay

423
00:41:45,743 --> 00:41:50,265
that contains
vital minerals scarce in their natural diet

424
00:41:50,386 --> 00:41:51,626
It may be mud

425
00:41:51,666 --> 00:41:56,589
but there's just
nothing quite like it for enriching the blood

426
00:41:57,710 --> 00:42:01,112
The clay also
helps to absorb the toxins

427
00:42:01,152 --> 00:42:04,313
found in many
leaves that the elephants eat

428
00:42:09,921 --> 00:42:12,803
There are other
benefits to coming here

429
00:42:13,845 --> 00:42:15,766
These clearings
are the only places

430
00:42:15,806 --> 00:42:18,409
where the forest
elephants can get together

431
00:42:18,449 --> 00:42:20,130
in such numbers

432
00:42:20,170 --> 00:42:22,412
When they
return to the forest

433
00:42:22,452 --> 00:42:26,215
they will have to
go their separate ways once more

434
00:42:47,093 --> 00:42:50,461
If large animals
are rare in jungles

435
00:42:50,501 --> 00:42:54,189
then groups of
large animals actually living together

436
00:42:54,229 --> 00:42:55,951
are even rarer

437
00:43:14,935 --> 00:43:17,343
This posse
of hunters

438
00:43:17,383 --> 00:43:19,229
is not
only formidable

439
00:43:19,269 --> 00:43:21,755
it's also
very large

440
00:43:41,366 --> 00:43:43,366
In their
search for food

441
00:43:43,406 --> 00:43:48,888
chimpanzees move effortlessly between the forest floor and the canopy

442
00:43:48,928 --> 00:43:52,449
They're one of
the few jungle animals able to do so

443
00:44:07,845 --> 00:44:11,926
Figs are a vital
part of a chimpanzee's diet

444
00:44:12,206 --> 00:44:15,727
And some just can'
t seem to get enough of them

445
00:44:25,848 --> 00:44:30,289
But there's something special about this stretch of forest in Uganda

446
00:44:30,329 --> 00:44:33,089
Fruit is
actually abundant

447
00:44:38,833 --> 00:44:43,527
And a lot of food
supports lots of chimps

448
00:45:00,337 --> 00:45:02,578
At 150 strong

449
00:45:02,618 --> 00:45:04,378
this community of chimps

450
00:45:04,418 --> 00:45:07,219
is the biggest
yet found in Africa

451
00:45:07,339 --> 00:45:09,059
Their numbers
are so large

452
00:45:09,099 --> 00:45:11,060
that they need
a big territory

453
00:45:11,100 --> 00:45:12,900
lots of fig trees

454
00:45:12,940 --> 00:45:15,181
and they're
willing to fight for it

455
00:45:24,809 --> 00:45:27,892
These calls
announce the start of a raid

456
00:45:27,932 --> 00:45:31,216
into land
controlled by their neighbours

457
00:45:37,384 --> 00:45:39,547
As they leave
their core zone

458
00:45:39,587 --> 00:45:41,669
the patrol
goes silent

459
00:45:41,709 --> 00:45:44,232
occasionally
stopping to listen

460
00:45:48,862 --> 00:45:50,467
Signs of the enemy

461
00:45:50,507 --> 00:45:51,792
are detected

462
00:45:51,832 --> 00:45:54,037
and examined closely

463
00:46:08,544 --> 00:46:10,187
The chimp militia

464
00:46:10,227 --> 00:46:13,791
are now at the
very edge of their territory

465
00:46:14,633 --> 00:46:17,478
All need to be
on maximum alert

466
00:46:17,518 --> 00:46:28,093
They must wait

467
00:46:28,254 --> 00:46:29,535
and listen

468
00:46:51,346 --> 00:46:55,553
An unfamiliar
chimp call raises the tension

469
00:46:55,634 --> 00:46:58,078
It's an
uncertain time

470
00:46:58,119 --> 00:47:02,327
The size of the
rival group is as yet unknown

471
00:47:05,567 --> 00:47:06,568
Not far away

472
00:47:06,608 --> 00:47:09,490
their neighbours
are feeding in a fig tree

473
00:47:09,571 --> 00:47:12,453
oblivious to the
approaching dangers

474
00:47:14,335 --> 00:47:16,217
The patrol
moves off

475
00:47:16,257 --> 00:47:18,099
with a
sense of purpose

476
00:47:18,139 --> 00:47:22,422
They must remain
silent until they close in on their rivals

477
00:47:34,493 --> 00:47:36,214
The attack is on

478
00:47:38,937 --> 00:47:40,858
To intimidate
their opponents

479
00:47:40,898 --> 00:47:44,621
the aggressors
scream and drum on buttress roots

480
00:48:05,291 --> 00:48:08,333
Several males
corner an enemy female

481
00:48:08,373 --> 00:48:10,214
It's a
ferocious attack

482
00:48:10,254 --> 00:48:12,976
and she's lucky
to escape with her life

483
00:48:30,674 --> 00:48:33,434
Others are
not so fortunate

484
00:48:39,795 --> 00:48:41,115
The battle won

485
00:48:41,155 --> 00:48:43,796
a grisly
scene unfolds

486
00:48:45,076 --> 00:48:48,516
An enemy youngster has been caught and killed

487
00:48:48,556 --> 00:48:52,117
The carcass is
shared between members of the group

488
00:48:52,157 --> 00:48:53,357
and eaten

489
00:48:57,334 --> 00:49:01,980
Killing a competitor makes sense if you want to protect your food supply

490
00:49:05,866 --> 00:49:10,671
But exactly why they cannibalise the dead chimp is not fully understood

491
00:49:12,154 --> 00:49:16,599
It may simply be
a chance for some extra protein

492
00:49:28,701 --> 00:49:32,663
Teamwork has brought this group of chimps great success

493
00:49:32,904 --> 00:49:36,226
But they'll soon
reach the limits of their power

494
00:49:37,708 --> 00:49:39,750
The competition for resources

495
00:49:39,790 --> 00:49:44,593
ensures that no
one species dominates the jungle

496
00:49:51,351 --> 00:49:55,595
The rainforest's
great diversity has come at a cost

497
00:49:55,716 --> 00:50:00,080
It has made them
the most finely balanced ecosystems in the world

498
00:50:00,120 --> 00:50:03,283
Only too easily
upset and destroyed

499
00:50:03,323 --> 00:50:05,245
By that
other great ape

500
00:50:05,285 --> 00:50:08,127
the chimpanzee's
closest relative

501
00:50:08,569 --> 00:50:09,809
ourselves

